l:\"triage\"`\n\tWorkers   Duration `yaml:\"workers\"`\n\tReviewers Duration `yaml:\"reviewers\"`\n\tMerger    Duration `yaml:\"merger\"`\n}\n\ntype intakeYAML struct {\n\tMode  IntakeMode `yaml:\"mode\"`\n\tLabel string     `yaml:\"label,omitempty\"`\n}\n\ntype staleYAML struct {\n\tInactiveFor Duration `yaml:\"inactive_for\"`\n\tSuppressFor Duration `yaml:\"suppress_for\"`\n}\n\ntype redactYAML struct {\n\tGitleaks gitleaksYAML `yaml:\"gitleaks\"`\n}\n\ntype gitleaksYAML struct {\n\tEnabled bool   `yaml:\"enabled\"`\n\tPath    string `yaml:\"path\"`\n\n\tBlockAutocloseWhenMissing bool `yaml:\"block_autoclose_when_missing\"`\n}\n\ntype sidecarYAML struct {\n\tRepo string `yaml:\"repo\"`\n\tRef  string `yaml:\"ref\"`\n}\n\ntype roleYAML struct {\n\tName       string   `yaml:\"name\"`\n\tKind       Kind     `yaml:\"kind\"`\n\tDir        string   `yaml:\"dir\"`\n\tPrompt     string   `yaml:\"prompt,omitempty\"`\n\tScan       string   `yaml:\"scan,omitempty\"`\n\tDockerfile string   `yaml:\"dockerfile,omitempty\"`\n\tExtra      []string `yaml:\"extra,omitempty\"`\n\n\tAgent   string   `yaml:\"agent\"`\n\tModel   string   `yaml:\"model,omitempty\"`\n\tTimeout Duration `yaml:\"timeout\"`\n\tSidecar string   `yaml:\"sidecar,omitempty\"`\n\n\tEvery     Duration `yaml:\"every,omitempty\"`\n\tMaxOpen   int      `yaml:\"max_open,omitempty\"`\n\tGroupSize int      `yaml:\"group_size,omitempty\"`\n\tAutoclose bool     `yaml:\"autoclose,omitempty\"`\n\tRoute     string   `yaml:\"route,omitempty\"`\n\n\tAfter       []string `yaml:\"after,omitempty\"`\n\tPlanSection string   `yaml:\"plan_section,omitempty\"`\n\n\tTests           string   `yaml:\"tests,omitempty\"`\n\tMaxTestFixes    int      `yaml:\"max_test_fixes,omitempty\"`\n\tMaxReviewRounds int      `yaml:\"max_review_rounds,omitempty\"`\ninternal/configcmd:\nconfig.go\nconfig_test.go\n\ninternal/triage:\ncandidates.go\ndoc.go\nfanin.go\nfanin_test.go\ngate_test.go\nharness_test.go\nintake.go\nintake_test.go\nloop.go\nloop_test.go\norder.go\norder_test.go\nrun.go\nsnapshot_test.go\n\ninternal/worker:\nbranch.go\ncandidates.go\ncandidates_test.go\ndoc.go\nfacts.go\nfeedback.go\nguard.go\nharness_test.go\nloop.go\nloop_test.go\npr.go\nrun.go\ntype.go\nunit_test.go\n190:4. **Diff.** A new, unsuppressed fingerprint is queued for filing. A suppressed fingerprint stays quiet unless its severity rises above the suppressed one. A fingerprint that returns after being fixed files a new issue that links the old one as a regression. An open issue whose fingerprints are all gone gets \"No longer detected at `<sha>`\" and is closed, but only for scanners marked `autoclose` (deterministic ones); other scanners never edit or close an issue for absence. A returning finding unticks its line.\n191:5. **Group.** Findings that share a `group` key join one issue, up to `group_size` (N), as a checklist with one line per finding (ticked lines count towards N). An empty `group` means one issue per finding. New findings join an open group issue of the same group that has room and is not yet claimed, meaning it carries no `ard:` label besides `ard:scanner:<name>` and the route's label (none under `route: none`); otherwise they start a new one. A regression never joins: it always opens a new issue, which may also hold new findings of the same group. The number of new issues per run is `min(max_open - open, global_max_open - global open)`; joins are free. Findings over that budget are filed most severe first, and the rest stay unrecorded so the next run offers them again.\n202:**Closed is not always \"forget\".** Removing a fingerprint on every close, as first sketched, refiles a won't-fix issue on the next run. Completed closes stop tracking the finding but keep a `fixed` tombstone, so a finding that comes back is filed as a regression of the old issue. Not-planned closes, and closes as a duplicate, suppress, and re-raise early if severity rises.\n221:**The cve scanner** (`scanners/cve`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program, no build step) merges osv-scanner (all ecosystems) and govulncheck (Go reachability). Its fingerprint is `osv:<advisory>:<manifest>:<package>`, where the advisory is the smallest `GHSA-` id of the vulnerability, else the smallest `CVE-` id, else the smallest id, so one vulnerability reported under `GO-`, `GHSA-` and `CVE-` ids is one finding. Severity is `critical|high|medium|low`, from the CVSS score, else the database severity, else `medium`; a vulnerability govulncheck finds unreachable is capped at `medium`. Manifests under `testdata/`, `vendor/` and `node_modules/` are skipped. It fails closed: if a Go module cannot be analysed, the run fails and files nothing, unless a human adds a `no-govulncheck` file to the role directory, which skips govulncheck and reports reachability as unknown. Each Go module is analysed with the image's Go toolchain unless its `go` directive, or a `toolchain` line, needs a newer one. Then the scanner lets Go pick that toolchain (`GOTOOLCHAIN=auto` rules: the `toolchain` line if present, else the `go` directive's minimum version), downloads it from the Go module proxy (checksum-verified; scanner jobs have egress) into its temp dir, rebuilds the same govulncheck version with it, and scans with both. The image sets `TMPDIR=/var/tmp` because the executor's `/tmp` is noexec. If the temp dir is noexec, or the toolchain cannot be downloaded or built, the run fails closed and names the reason. Findings from such a module say which toolchain was scanned (`Go toolchain:` line), and standard-library findings are measured against it (for `go 1.27`, `go1.27.0`), not against the image's Go. The Dependabot alerts API is not wrapped, because a scanner holds no token.\n227:**The missing-tests scanner** (`scanners/missing-tests`, a `sh` script plus a stdlib-only Go helper) runs each Go module's own tests with `go test -coverpkg=./...` and files one finding per exported function or method that no test calls by name or that the tests never run; the group is the package, and the fingerprint does not depend on the file or line. Because it runs the repository's tests it needs the executor's container and egress to the module proxy. If a module's tests fail to build or fail, the scan fails and files nothing, so autoclose never acts on partial coverage. A human-owned `ignore` file next to `scan` skips symbols or packages, and a `no-coverage` file there skips the test run and reports only the static rule. It ships in the starter tree but is not enabled on ard's own `.ard/`.\n278:An executor runs one agent job somewhere and reports back; the controller does not care where. Both executors implement the same five calls: `prepare`, `start`, `status`, `logs`, `cleanup`. Prepare also removes any `Job.Scrub` paths (the job's own role file, directly under `.ard/<issue#>/`) from the fresh worktree before the agent starts. Job IDs are ULIDs minted at claim time; they name the worktree and the logs and appear as the `Ard-Job` commit trailer. The executor captures the job's stdout separately from its combined log, because the adapter parses one and the committed log comes from the other. Scanner jobs get network egress for advisory databases; agent jobs get only their model credential.\n330:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (2,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 1,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages and uses no other network, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make test`, and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 1000`, `diff_max_lines: 2000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base.\n460:- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token; in Kubernetes a network policy limits egress to the model API and package registries.\ndeploy/charts/ard-serve/Chart.yaml\ndeploy/charts/ard-serve/templates/pvc.yaml\ndeploy/charts/ard-serve/templates/externalsecret.yaml\ndeploy/charts/ard-serve/templates/networkpolicy.yaml","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c1cc8f51-1d78-4afd-811d-bd4da46c02f4","uuid":"0addc847-da67-4f3b-8229-29e63e1fa7c4","timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:58:42.478Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"internal/config/types.go:123:\tTimeout time.Duration // default 30m\ninternal/config/types.go:141:\tTests           string   // default \"make test\"\npackage config\n\nimport (\n\t\"io\"\n\n\t\"gopkg.in/yaml.v3\"\n)\n\n// WriteYAML renders the resolved configuration to w in the same schema\n// .ard/config.yaml uses, so `a